/**

@page page_exahype_gpu GPU support

@tableofcontents


@image html documentation/exahype2/gpus.png

With a GPU backend, every solver stores its cell and face data in pools in
device memory, and every phase of a time step runs as kernels over the cell,
face and level-jump spans of the cell connectivity. The host reads data back for file
output, for initial conditions that read host objects, for the refinement votes
of a step that asks for a regrid, and for the reduced error norms.

To run a solver on the GPU:

1. @ref page_installation_home "Configure" Peano with a GPU backend.
2. Provide PDE terms that run on the device, as described below.
3. Run some @ref page_exahype_runtime_analysis "performance analysis".
4. Study the @ref page_exahype_performance_optimisation "optimisation recommendations".


## Using ExaHyPE with GPU support

The setup instructions for the GPU backends are collected on
@ref page_compiler_specific_settings_home "the compiler-specific settings page".
This page describes how a time step runs on the GPU, which GPU a rank uses, and
what a PDE term needs to run on the device.


## How a time step runs on the GPU

tarch::accelerator::parallelFor and tarch::accelerator::parallelReduce launch one
kernel per dispatch. One dispatch covers every cell or face of a span. A time
step then runs a fixed sequence of kernels (@ref page_exahype_multicore). The
halo exchange gathers the halves of the exchanged faces into one buffer in
device memory. MPI sends from and receives into device memory. A run with more
than one rank needs a GPU-aware MPI library. The configuration summary reports
whether the library is GPU-aware.

A GPU pays off when a rank owns enough cells to fill the device. Every dispatch
costs a kernel launch and a synchronisation. A rank with few cells leaves the
GPU idle for most of a time step. The MPI decomposition rows of the grid report
give the cells per rank.


## Several GPUs

tarch::init() gives every MPI rank one GPU. The ranks of a node take the GPUs of
the node in turn. The rank with number r on its node takes GPU r modulo the
number of GPUs. Every allocation and every kernel of the rank uses that GPU. A
rank that sees one GPU only takes that GPU, for example through
`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` or a batch system that binds one GPU per task. Two ranks
on one GPU both store their data in its memory. The GPU interleaves their
kernels.


## Provide the PDE terms without state

A PDE term runs on the device as a function without state. It reads its
arguments, global constants and runtime parameters. It cannot modify the solver
object.

A runtime parameter stores a value that changes from run to run.
`project.add_runtime_parameter(name, type, default)` registers it. The
executable reads it from `config.json` and the command line. Every term receives
the generated struct `RuntimeParameters` as its last argument `parameters` and
reads the value as `parameters.<name>`. The struct also stores the grid
parameters `maxAdmissiblePatchSize`, `minAdmissiblePatchSize`, `gridLevels` and
`maxGridLevels`. Code that `on_start_simulation()` or
`on_start_grid_initialisation()` inserts may overwrite a value of `parameters`.
Every later term reads the new value.

The Python API generates these terms. `set_pde_terms()` of most ExaHyPE solvers
takes the C++ body of a term. The generated solver class emits that body as a
`static` method with the annotation `GPU_CALLABLE_METHOD`. The host and the
device then call the same method. The application defines a term given as
`True`. An application that registers `<Name>.cpph` with
`project.output.makefile.add_h_file("<Name>.cpph")` defines these terms inline in
that file. The generated `<Name>.h` then declares every device term with
`INLINE_METHOD GPU_CALLABLE_METHOD`. A term that `<Name>.cpp` defines runs on the
host only. `src/tarch/accelerator/CompileTarget.h` defines the macros:

- `GPU_CALLABLE_METHOD` expands to the appropriate per-backend annotation
  (`__host__ __device__` for CUDA/HIP, `KOKKOS_FUNCTION` for Kokkos,
  `ACPP_UNIVERSAL_TARGET` for AdaptiveCpp and an empty expansion for DPC++).
- `TARGET_DECLARE` and `TARGET_DECLARE_END` wrap a block of free functions in an
  `omp declare target` region of the OpenMP target (OMPT) backend. Every other
  backend defines them as empty macros.

An `ASSERTION` in a term on the device aborts the run of a build with
assertions. Each lowering into Peano writes a README.md file. It lists the
papers that have an influence on the chosen solver configuration.


## Compile, run and check

The build system is CMake. Configure Peano and the ExaHyPE libraries with a GPU
backend. The relevant flags are
`PEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND` (one of `none`, `native`, `kokkos`, `acpp`, `ompt`)
and `PEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH` (e.g. an NVIDIA `sm_*`, an AMD `gfx*`, or `spir64`
for Intel). A typical configuration reads:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
         -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=native \
         -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=sm_80
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Pick the backend that matches your toolchain as described on the
@ref page_compiler_specific_settings_home "compiler-specific settings page".
Once Peano is rebuilt, the generated solver is GPU ready: Rerun the Python
script to lower your project, then compile and run.


## Further reading

- Switch to a toolchain for your particular GPU backend. Information on this can
  be found on the @ref page_compiler_specific_settings_home "compiler-specific settings page".
- Consult the @ref page_exahype_performance_optimisation "performance optimisation" discussion.
- @ref page_tarch_tasking "The tasking layer in the technical architecture".

  */
